  4. WHY THIS MODULE IS IN SCOPE: THREE STRANDS
  .............................................
  4.1 It is the module where the JDK 17 build probe first failed. A trial
      build on the target runtime terminated here with a missing package
      error naming javax.xml.bind, at precise coordinates across five main
      source files in this module alone, verified as eight occurrences:
      LocalDateFrevvoAdapter.java:32, DateFrevvoAdapter.java:32,
      DateMillisecondAdapter.java:30, XMLUnmarshaller.java:42, :43, :44,
      and XMLMarshaller.java:38, :39. The fixture method of this very test
      class constructs the two marshalling collaborators directly, so the
      class cannot even load without that API present.
      That makes this module the archive's canonical evidence that the
      reinstated API remedy works. The enterprise modules removed from the
      platform runtime are restored as ordinary javax namespace Maven
      dependencies declared ONCE in the root aggregator's global dependency
      block, which propagates them by inheritance to all 144 child modules
      and reproduces the ubiquity the old platform runtime provided,
      leaving all 84 consuming files with ZERO import edits. If this module
      compiles and its report reappears after the migration, the remedy is
      demonstrated at its hardest point.
  4.2 The two structural special cases described in sections 1 and 6, the
      default package and the live timestamp standard output. The archive
      must represent both correctly or else the layout contract and the
      comparison methodology are misstated.
  4.3 The class extends the EasyMock support base type, so this report is
      also evidence for the mocking library floor advance: the installed
      release and the intermediate one both fail identically on the target
      runtime with an inaccessible object error raised from that library's
      own class proxy factory, and only a much later release succeeds. That
      library has the widest POM footprint of any change in the migration.

  5. NAMESPACE INVARIANCE
  .......................
  Every javax name stays a javax name. The platform removal is compensated
  by ADDING javax namespace artifacts, never by rewriting source. Artifact
  eligibility was decided by inspecting jar package prefixes rather than
  coordinates, so an artifact merely NAMED for the successor namespace is
  rejected even when its own classes are javax prefixed, because carrying
  it would place a successor named jar on the class path and duplicate its
  class set. Writing javax.xml.bind in this file is correct and required.
  No successor namespace token appears anywhere in this file. This is the
  highest risk file in the archive for that gate, because its whole
  narrative concerns the removed XML binding API.

